# PII denylist — EXAMPLE. Copy to $LIFE_AGENT_KB/pii-patterns.txt and fill in.
#
#   cp config/pii-patterns.txt.example "$LIFE_AGENT_KB/pii-patterns.txt"
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   .githooks/pii_check.py is a structural guard for this PUBLIC repo. It already
#   catches *shaped* PII automatically (emails on non-allowlisted domains,
#   checksum-valid national IDs, passport/phone shapes, personal file paths).
#   This file supplements that with the SHAPELESS things only you know: the
#   literal names, employers, and private domains that would identify you but
#   match no generic pattern.
#
# HOW IT IS READ
#   One regex per line (Python `re`, case-insensitive). Blank lines and lines
#   beginning with `#` are ignored. A commit/push is blocked if any tracked line
#   matches — the matched value is NEVER printed, only `path:line: kind`.
#
# WHERE IT LIVES
#   In $LIFE_AGENT_KB (outside this repo), because the patterns themselves are
#   personal. The guard runs "fail-closed": in its default mode it refuses to
#   scan if this file is missing, so copy it before your first commit. (For an
#   ad-hoc scan without it, use `python .githooks/pii_check.py --shapes-only`.)
#
# The entries below are SYNTHETIC placeholders. Delete them and add your own.

# A private surname that appears in your documents but nowhere public:
Zzyzx

# A maiden name or family name (word-boundaried so it doesn't over-match):
\bQwerty\b

# An employer or org name that would identify you:
Acme Widgets (Pty) Ltd

# A personal domain you own (escape the dots):
my-private-domain\.example

# A street name from your address:
Nonesuch Avenue
